[ti:2-02-城市化]
[00:00.00]The chart above clearly reveals the degree of urbani-zation in China during the past 40 years.
[00:08.46]From 1980 to 2010,
[00:10.06]it jumped markedly from 19.39% to 49.95%,
[00:13.81]a rise of approximately 30%.
[00:16.38]The percentage increased steadily from 49.95% to 63.89% between 2010 and 2020,
[00:23.56]a rise of nearly 14%.
[00:25.38]It goes without saying that remarkable achievements have been made in China’s urbanization since the reform and opening- up in the past few decades.
[00:38.77]First and foremost,
[00:40.59]the fundamental contributor for such a sharp increase in urban population results from China’s economic prosperity.
[00:51.30]Furthermore,
[00:52.59]urbanization has created ample job opportunities for ordinary people,
[00:59.12]especially for those migrant workers who move to urban areas.
[01:04.69]More importantly,
[01:06.41]the authorities concerned have substantially increased infrastructure facilities in urban areas,
[01:15.62]such as recreational parks and cultural centers,
[01:20.12]to satisfy the requirements of urban dwellers.
[01:24.40]Even so,
[01:25.15]it is also worth mentioning that as people in mounting numbers enjoy the prosperity of modern cities,
[01:34.26]we cannot ignore the increasing challenges urbanization has posed to natural resources,
[01:42.40]the environment,
[01:44.01]job market and public services.
